  "step_2": "The individual notes that both lotteries have a very low chance of winning, but not completely unrealistic. The numbers are nearly identical, so the decision was dominated by whether the 100€ gain in winning probability (from 33% to 34%) was worth the cost of 100€ in prize money. The individual concludes that, given the high risk of getting nothing, it was more valuable to reduce that risk (by increasing the chance of winning) than to gain 100€ in the case of winning. The deliberation focuses on risk aversion and the value of reducing the probability of losing everything. There is no mention of aspiration levels, mean outcomes, or differences from a mean. The justification is based on risk mitigation and the perceived value of avoiding loss, not on comparing positive deviations from a mean aspiration level. The individual does not refer to any calculation involving the mean of outcomes or the probability of positive differences relative to that mean.",
  "step_3": "The information relevant to the decision reason — the calculation of aspiration levels (1200 for A, 1250 for B), the positive differences (1200 and 1250), and the comparison of the probability of positive differences (34% vs. 33%) — is not present in the verbal report. The individual does not mention the mean of outcomes or deviations from it. Instead, the justification centers on risk aversion and the value of increasing the chance of winning (even by a small margin) to avoid the risk of losing everything. This reflects a different decision rationale (risk aversion or risk mitigation), not the aspiration-level-based rule. Therefore, the individual did not use the decision reason. The reasoning in the report is conceptually distinct from the aspiration-level rule. Final assessment: 0.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
}

